b/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488177 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488177 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe90978e-57e2-5d2c-a9e0-a9c95c24e6df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5e39b8db15f47b0c07efec1b364f6fda7b3b7991ba13d71125505b1172937888.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28cb24da-7ead-51cb-82fe-c700e5c2c25f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488198 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{2}\\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "506b27d0-9ecb-54b1-8cb0-ba21f98fbff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488208 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, so $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/40c6f3bbfcbf08db07501076a43ba011da20c57c70865ec2700334d560936eae.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56f49ac3-e6b5-56aa-af36-053b89a652ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488210 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $D,$ defined as\n\n$$\nD=\\{n-{n}^{2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $D?$\n\n$inf(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73aef377-5ed1-5df9-8acf-ea492a3b5fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488226 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a set $D$ is the greatest lower bound of $D.$\n\nIf $D$ is a finite set, the infimum of $D$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $D:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3-{3}^{2} & =-6 \\\\ 4-{4}^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(D) & =min\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/81/8152677cc952ca219aa9395f3d1a9acbc9379b1cffd0b0f0e6bd0f3f0148632a.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75529c50-5084-5200-a1d5-34d0fbf018a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488227 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the supremum of the set $C=\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}?$\n\n$sup(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b620bc4-f772-542c-8902-9f29e69330eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488237 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the supremum of $C$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(C) & =max\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1)\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d021d0f6-fbf3-5eed-804d-eee551071be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488264 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/58/5812e3dd22994d613ecc84087418cddfa978e98c1324869f290e1bf2b1317bad.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded above. In other words, for any real number $x,$ we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ such that ${a}_{n}$ is greater than $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no upper bounds and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B=[-1,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d48b0cc5-05e8-55c3-b441-64611554a8ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488268 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the minimum of $B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=-1\\in B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/50b52da574101dfca7c620411a446f2c5f3ef41242186ca38da072e140019024.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddf4ec2c-4f4d-56c1-bc14-78cfd2f52940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488271 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-2,3].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<3$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $3,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[3,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[3,\\infty )=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
